408→                           Erdős (unpublished) proved that this is true if $A$ has infinite measure, or if $A$ is an unbounded set of positive measure (stating in <a href="#cite-Er78d-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er78d','352'); return false;">[Er78d]</a> and <a href="#cite-Er83d-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er83d','352'); return false;">[Er83d]</a> it 'follows easily from the Lebesgue density theorem'). <br><br>In <a href="#cite-Er78d-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er78d','352'); return false;">[Er78d]</a> and <a href="#cite-Er83d-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er83d','352'); return false;">[Er83d]</a> he speculated that perhaps $C=4\pi/\sqrt{27}\approx 2.418$ works, which would be the best possible, as witnessed by a circle of radius $&lt;2\cdot 3^{-3/4}$.<br><br>Further evidence for this is given by a result of Freiling and Mauldin <a href="#cite-Ma02-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ma02','352'); return false;">[Ma02]</a>, who proved that if $A$ has outer measure $&gt;4\pi/\sqrt{27}$ then $A$ contains the vertices of a triangle with area $&gt;1$. This also proves the same threshold for the original problem under the assumption that $A$ is a compact convex set. <br><br>Mauldin also discusses this problem in <a href="#cite-Ma13-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ma13','352'); return false;">[Ma13]</a>, in which he notes that it suffices to prove this under the assumption that $A$ is the union of the interiors of $n&lt;\infty$ many compact convex sets. Freiling and Mauldin (see <a href="#cite-Ma13-352" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ma13','352'); return false;">[Ma13]</a>) have proved this conjecture if $1\leq n\leq 3$.
